  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   - Rationale: The core idea is to represent the function h(x) in terms of its Fourier coefficients, which allows us to capture periodicity and smoothness efficiently. We condense the problem's high-dimensional grid representation into a much lower-dimensional Fourier representation (21 variables for n=360). This approach aims to automatically satisfy the sum constraint and explore a wider range of candidates due to reduced dimensionality.\n   - Central idea: Optimize over Fourier coefficients to construct a function that minimizes overlap while maintaining integral 1 and bounds [0,1].\n   - Breaks the high-dimensional non-convex problem: By focusing on a few dominant frequencies, we sidestep local optima. The fixed smoothing parameter \u03b1=50 smooths the non-differentiable max, enabling gradient-based optimization. Cross-validating on multiple resolutions (360, 720) helps avoid overfitting.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Representation: Fourier series truncated at 10 harmonics: h(x) = a\u2080/2 + \u2211\u2096\u208c\u2081\u00b9\u2070 [a\u2096 cos(2\u03c0kx) + b\u2096 sin(2\u03c0kx)]\n   - Objective: Minimize C5 = (2/n) * max\u2096 \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx (objective function)\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1, \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n   - Variable reduction: 21 decision variables (a\u2080, b\u2081..b\u2081\u2080, a\u2081..a\u2081\u2080)\n   - Smoothed objective: C\u2085,\u03b1(x) = max\u2070.\u00b9\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx + (1/\u03b1)log(\u2211\u2c7cexp(\u03b1(c\u2096 - max\u2099c\u2098))), where max\u2099c\u2098 is the true max\n   - Solvers: sci.optimize.minimize (method='L-BFGS-B') with Jacobian computed by automatic differentiation\n   - Initialization: For resolutions (720, 360):\n        - Use Fourier basis initialization (random initial Fourier coefficients)\n        - Add sinusoidal bases (frequencies [2,3,4,5,7])\n        - Include transfer of parent solution (n=360)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Step 1: Validate parent solution if input exists\n   - Step 2: Hierarchy of resolutions (720 \u2193 360) and templates:\n        ```\n        templates = {'sinewave', 'gaussian', 'rectangular', 'random', 'parent_transfer'}\n        ```\n   - Step 3: For each resolution, do:\n        - Initialize 8 candidates (combining templates without repeats)\n        - Apply smooth_max optimization (Adam variant) to minimize C5\n        - Use coordinate descent (strong local search)\n        - Monitor improvement and set x\u2080 for next resolution\n   - Step 4: Manage time budget (evaluate 1300s):\n        ```\n        Early stopping: If candidate doesn't improve, abort\n        Dynamic adaptive resolution: Transfer candidates from coarser grid\n        Budget guard: Total time for each resolution < 200s\n        ```\n   - Step 5: Project feasible solution to grid and clip values\n\n4. Search Choices\n   - Primary representation: Fourier series truncated at k=10\n   - Initializations:\n        - Many candidates at each resolution\n        - Dynamic downsampling to avoid redundancy\n        - Parent solution always included\n   - Optimization:\n        - Adam optimizer with 500 max iterations\n        - Stricter tolerance on function improvement\n\n5. Compute Budget & Robustness\n   - Estimate function < 50ms (4 candidates/min/\u03b1=50)\n   - Time budget breakdown:\n        - Smooth optimization: 1000s\n        - Coordinated descent: 300s\n        - Cross-resolution: 500s\n        - Other: 500s\n   - Resolution mapping:\n        - n=720 \u2192 optimization in 42 D\n        - n=360 \u2192 optimization in 21 D\n   - Fallback procedure if stuck: Decrease resolution and reinit\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Project after smooth_max and clip:\n        ```\n        h_grid = np.clip(grid_vector, 0, 1)\n        h_grid = project_to_sum(h_grid, n_points/2)\n        ```\n   - Check for NaNs and finite C5 value\n   - Recompute objective with scipy.correlate to verify\n   - Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n   - Ryan point: If no progress, return parent solution (n=360)",
